sec.p:
Polypropylene discs were placed into individual wells of sterile 96-well microtiter plates to support biofilm growth. A C. tropicalis suspension was prepared in TSB and adjusted to an OD₆₀₀ corresponding to 1 × 10⁶ cells mL⁻1, confirmed using a hemocytometer. One hundred microliters of this standardized culture was dispensed into each well containing a disc and allowed to attach for 90 min at 37 °C. After the adhesion period, unattached cells were gently removed by rinsing with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS). Fresh TSB, adjusted to the required pH levels (5.4–7.8), was then added; and the plates were incubated under varying experimental conditions, which include different ranges of temperature, incubation period, and shaking speeds as reported in our previous work (Yogendraiah et al. 2025). Following incubation, biofilm formation was quantified by CV assay, with absorbance recorded at 570 nm (Sadanandan et al. 2022b). Finally, the data was analyzed using a second-order regression model to generate a predictive equation based on the coefficients of each experimental variable as shown in Eq. 1.1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
